    title: "What is Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and why use it?",
    summary: "A pattern where you retrieve relevant documents from your own data and inject them into the prompt so the LLM can answer using up-to-date, private information.",
    explanation: "LLMs only know what they were trained on and have no access to your company's private documents or recent data by default. RAG solves this by first searching a knowledge base (usually via vector similarity search) for chunks of text relevant to the user's question, then including those chunks in the prompt as context before asking the model to answer. This grounds the model's response in real, retrievable source material, reduces hallucination compared to relying purely on the model's memorized knowledge, and lets you update the knowledge base without retraining or fine-tuning anything.",
    code: "async function answerWithRAG(question) {\n  const relevantChunks = await vectorSearch(question, { topK: 5 });\n  const context = relevantChunks.map((c) => c.text).join(\"\\n\\n\");\n  const response = await client.messages.create({\n    model: \"claude-opus-4-8\",\n    max_tokens: 1024,\n    messages: [{\n      role: \"user\",\n      content: `Answer using only the context below.\\n\\nContext:\\n${context}\\n\\nQuestion: ${question}`,\n    }],\n  });\n  return response.content[0].text;\n}",
    interviewQuestion: "Why is RAG usually preferred over fine-tuning when you need an LLM to answer questions about your company's internal documentation?",
