    topic: "RAG for App Developers",
    title: "How does chunking strategy affect RAG quality, and what tradeoffs exist?",
    summary: "Chunk size and boundaries determine what context the retriever can find and how much noise gets included alongside relevant information.",
    explanation: "Chunks that are too small lose surrounding context needed to make sense of a fact (e.g. a table row without its header), while chunks too large dilute the embedding's specificity and waste context window tokens on irrelevant text, pushing costs and latency up and sometimes burying the relevant sentence among unrelated ones. Semantic chunking (splitting on headings, paragraphs, or using an LLM to identify logical boundaries) generally outperforms naive fixed-size splitting for structured documents, at the cost of more preprocessing complexity. Overlapping chunks (e.g. 10-20% overlap) help prevent a single relevant fact from being split awkwardly across a chunk boundary and lost. There's no universal best chunk size; it should be tuned against your actual document types and evaluated against a test set of representative questions.",
    code: "function chunkBySections(markdown, maxTokens = 400) {\n  const sections = markdown.split(/\\n(?=#{1,3} )/); // split on markdown headings\n  const chunks = [];\n  for (const section of sections) {\n    if (estimateTokens(section) <= maxTokens) {\n      chunks.push(section);\n    } else {\n      // fall back to fixed-size splitting with overlap for oversized sections\n      chunks.push(...splitWithOverlap(section, maxTokens, 0.15));\n    }\n  }\n  return chunks;\n}",
