    summary: "Balance retrieval quality, embedding dimensionality (storage/compute cost), and whether the model matches your content's language and domain.",
    explanation: "Higher-dimensional embeddings often capture more nuance but cost more to store and search; many modern embedding models let you truncate dimensions (Matryoshka-style embeddings) to trade off a bit of accuracy for significantly less storage. You should evaluate embedding models on a representative sample of your own queries and documents rather than relying purely on generic benchmarks like MTEB, since domain-specific text (legal, medical, code) may perform very differently across models. It's also important that you always use the same model (and ideally the same version) for both ingestion and query-time embedding, since vectors from different models are not comparable.",
    code: "// Using a truncated-dimension embedding to save storage while keeping most quality\nconst res = await openai.embeddings.create({\n  model: \"text-embedding-3-large\",\n  input: text,\n  dimensions: 256, // truncated from the default 3072\n});\nconst vector = res.data[0].embedding;",
