    id: "aifordevs-vector-db-vs-pgvector",
    category: "aifordevs",
    difficulty: "Intermediate",
    topic: "Vector Databases for Developers",
    title: "When would you choose pgvector over a dedicated vector database like Pinecone?",
    summary: "pgvector adds vector search to Postgres, which is a great fit when you already have relational data and don't need extreme scale; dedicated vector DBs shine at very high scale or advanced ANN tuning.",
    explanation: "Using pgvector inside an existing Postgres database avoids operational overhead (one less system to run, backup, and secure), lets you join vector search results with relational data in a single query, and keeps transactional consistency between your app data and your vectors. Dedicated vector databases typically offer more advanced indexing options, better horizontal scaling for very large vector counts, and features like automatic replication across regions. The general guidance is: start with pgvector if you're already on Postgres and your scale is moderate (up to tens of millions of vectors); migrate to a dedicated vector database if you hit performance ceilings, need multi-region low-latency search, or need advanced filtering/hybrid search features pgvector doesn't yet support well.",
    code: "-- pgvector: vector search joined directly with relational data\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;\n\nCREATE TABLE documents (\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  user_id INT REFERENCES users(id),\n  content TEXT,\n  embedding VECTOR(1536)\n);\n\nCREATE INDEX ON documents USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops);\n\nSELECT d.content, u.email\nFROM documents d\nJOIN users u ON u.id = d.user_id\nWHERE d.user_id = 42\nORDER BY d.embedding <=> $1\nLIMIT 5;",
    interviewQuestion: "Your app already stores structured order data in Postgres and now needs semantic search over product descriptions. Would you reach for pgvector or a standalone vector database, and why?",
