    id: "aifordevs-ai-api-error-handling",
    category: "aifordevs",
    difficulty: "Intermediate",
    topic: "AI API Error Handling",
    title: "What error categories should an app handle differently when calling an LLM API?",
    summary: "Distinguish retryable errors (rate limits, server overload, network issues) from non-retryable ones (invalid request, auth failure, content policy refusal) and handle each appropriately.",
    explanation: "Treating every API failure the same way — either retrying blindly or failing immediately — leads to either wasted retries on errors that will never succeed, or giving up too early on transient issues that would resolve with a brief backoff. HTTP 429 (rate limited) and 5xx server errors are generally retryable with exponential backoff; HTTP 400 (malformed request) and 401/403 (auth issues) are not retryable and indicate a bug or misconfiguration that retrying won't fix. A model refusing to answer for safety/policy reasons is a distinct case — it's a successful API call by HTTP status, but the content indicates the model declined, which your app should handle as a UX case (explain to the user, offer to rephrase) rather than a technical error. Most official SDKs provide typed exception classes for these categories so you can branch on them precisely instead of parsing error message strings, which are not a stable API contract.",
    code: "async function callWithErrorHandling(params) {\n  try {\n    return await client.messages.create(params);\n  } catch (err) {\n    if (err instanceof Anthropic.RateLimitError) {\n      await sleep(err.headers?.[\"retry-after\"] * 1000 || 5000);\n      return callWithErrorHandling(params); // retryable\n    }\n    if (err instanceof Anthropic.AuthenticationError) {\n      throw new Error(\"AI service misconfigured — check API key\"); // non-retryable, alert engineers\n    }\n    if (err instanceof Anthropic.BadRequestError) {\n      throw new Error(\"Invalid request to AI service\"); // non-retryable, likely a code bug\n    }\n    throw err; // unknown, surface it\n  }\n}",
