    title: "What should you log and monitor specifically for an LLM-powered feature, beyond standard application logging?",
    summary: "Full prompts and responses (with PII handling), token usage and cost per request, latency per call, model/prompt version, and downstream quality signals like user feedback or eval scores.",
    explanation: "Standard application logging (errors, request duration) is necessary but not sufficient for LLM features, because the 'correctness' of a response isn't something a status code tells you — you need visibility into what was actually sent to the model and what came back to debug quality issues, not just crashes. Logging full prompts/responses raises privacy considerations (PII, sensitive content), so many teams log with redaction, sampling, or a shorter retention window for raw content while keeping aggregate metrics (token counts, latency, cost, model version) longer-term. Tracking which prompt version and model version produced a given response is essential for correlating quality regressions with deploys. Layering in downstream signals — thumbs up/down feedback, whether a user had to retry/rephrase, or automated eval scores on a sample of production traffic — turns your logs from a debugging tool into an ongoing quality-monitoring system that can catch silent regressions (like the model provider updating a model version under the hood) before they become widespread complaints.",
    code: "async function loggedGenerate(params, metadata) {\n  const start = Date.now();\n  try {\n    const response = await client.messages.create(params);\n    await logAIRequest({\n      promptVersion: metadata.promptVersion,\n      model: params.model,\n      inputTokens: response.usage.input_tokens,\n      outputTokens: response.usage.output_tokens,\n      latencyMs: Date.now() - start,\n      requestId: response.id,\n      userId: metadata.userId,\n      // store a redacted/truncated snippet, not always the full raw content\n      promptPreview: redactPII(params.messages.at(-1).content).slice(0, 200),\n    });\n    return response;\n  } catch (err) {\n    await logAIError({ error: err.message, latencyMs: Date.now() - start, ...metadata });\n    throw err;\n  }\n}",
