    id: "aifordevs-ai-workflows-pipelines",
    category: "aifordevs",
    difficulty: "Advanced",
    topic: "Building AI Workflows/Pipelines",
    title: "How do you design a multi-step AI workflow versus a single monolithic prompt?",
    summary: "Break a complex task into a pipeline of smaller, focused LLM calls (or LLM + deterministic code steps) chained together, each easier to test, debug, and improve independently.",
    explanation: "A single giant prompt trying to do everything at once (research, analyze, format, verify) tends to be less reliable and much harder to debug than a pipeline where each step has one clear responsibility. A common pattern: a 'generation' step produces a first draft, a separate 'verification' or 'critique' step checks it against requirements, and a 'refinement' step fixes issues, each with its own focused prompt rather than one prompt trying to self-critique inline. Deterministic code steps (parsing, validation, database lookups) are interleaved with LLM steps where appropriate, rather than asking the LLM to do work a simple function could do more reliably and cheaply. This decomposition also lets you use different, appropriately-sized models per step (a cheap model for simple sub-tasks, an expensive one only for the step that truly needs it), and makes it possible to write focused evals for each pipeline stage independently rather than only being able to evaluate the whole system end-to-end.",
