    explanation: "Because LLM behavior is probabilistic and quality can be subtly worse in ways that only show up at scale (edge cases, adversarial inputs, rare input distributions), shipping an AI feature or prompt change to 100% of users immediately is riskier than for typical deterministic code changes. A staged rollout — internal dogfooding, then a small percentage of real users, then gradual ramp-up — lets you catch regressions on real traffic before they affect everyone, using the same feature-flagging infrastructure teams already use for other product rollouts. A/B testing a new model or prompt version against the current production version, measured against both automated eval scores and real user engagement/satisfaction signals, gives you confidence the change is actually an improvement rather than just different. Because model providers can also change model behavior server-side (deprecations, silent updates to a model alias), an AI feature rollout system should support pinning to specific model versions where the provider allows it, and always maintain a fast kill-switch to fall back to a previous known-good prompt/model configuration if a regression is detected post-launch.",
